---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-drip';
const title = 'Privy vs Drip for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = "Privy vs Drip for Shopify: compare replenishment logic, capture, workflow maintenance and operator handover using a proposed coffee reorder exercise.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "What should decide between Privy and Drip?",
    "answer": "Use the reorder policy, required Shopify data and operator handover. Monthly revenue does not establish a universal winner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Privy replace Drip for automation?",
    "answer": "It may satisfy your requirements. Build the actual branches and queued-send cancellation test rather than assuming an order-count ceiling."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should pricing be compared at 10,000 contacts?",
    "answer": "Obtain matching quotes for eligible contacts, sends, channels, support and any integration work. Include ongoing rule maintenance."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I use both?",
    "answer": "Only when a specific capture benefit justifies the handoff. Prove source and preference sync and give each flow one owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I also consider Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "Trial it against the same product, gift and subscription cases if the first two do not fit. No performance or setup advantage is established here."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which workflow builder is better?",
    "answer": "Compare the ability to inspect queued recipients and change a rule safely. A visual diagram alone does not establish correctness."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which has better popup tools?",
    "answer": "Both offer capture options. Compare equivalent offers, mobile behavior and promised-content delivery on your storefront."
  },
  {
    "question": "What workflow test should decide?",
    "answer": "Use a replenishment journey with gift and subscription exclusions, a second purchase during the wait and a controlled delay edit."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Privy vs Drip</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs Drip for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">For a coffee store, a reorder journey is useful only if it can distinguish products, avoid subscription duplicates and stop when a new purchase arrives. Privy and Drip both deserve evaluation for marketing automation and capture. Compare how clearly an operator can express and maintain your actual reorder policy instead of assigning one platform an arbitrary order-volume ceiling.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 text-cyan-100/90">This is a requirements comparison with proposed exercises, not a hands-on performance study. SMS scope, destination eligibility and support need confirmation for the selected account when used.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Privy</th><th>Drip</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Primary trial</td><td>Build capture and reorder messages together</td><td>Build the same journey using Shopify data</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Capture</td><td>Evaluate the current display tools</td><td>Evaluate forms and popups as well as email</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Replenishment</td><td>Demonstrate the required product inputs</td><td>Demonstrate data availability and workflow rules</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Edit delays and exclusions safely</td><td>Hand the workflow to a second operator</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Stack choice</td><td>May own the whole tested journey</td><td>May own it alone or receive capture from another tool</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Both products extend beyond the old capture-versus-canvas split</h2>
        <p>Drip publishes a Shopify offering with email workflows, segmentation and popups. Privy markets automation alongside email, SMS and list growth. Neither positioning proves support for every product attribute in your catalog. The useful question is whether your operator can build the specific rule without an undocumented spreadsheet or fragile manual tag.</p>
        <h2>Proposed exercise: coffee replenishment with exceptions</h2>
        <p>Create test orders for a small bag, a large bag, a gift purchase and a subscription customer. Give each a written policy: the gift purchase receives brewing guidance rather than a refill offer; the subscription customer is excluded from one-off reorder promotion. Choose delays from your own purchase history and product assumptions. These are proposed fixtures, not measured buyer behavior.</p>
        <h2>Check the input before drawing the branch</h2>
        <p>Inspect the actual customer and order records in each trial. Confirm whether bag size, subscription state and gift designation are present and usable. A Shopify metafield existing in the store does not prove it is exposed to a journey condition. If an integration must copy it to a custom field, record who updates it and how missing or stale values are handled.</p>
        <h2>Test a second order during the waiting period</h2>
        <p>Place another coffee order before the original reminder is due. Require a clear policy: cancel the old reminder, restart the clock or retain a specific education message. Repeat with a refund and a mixed cart. Inspect the queued recipient rather than trusting only the workflow diagram. A readable canvas matters when it corresponds to the data and pending sends an operator can inspect.</p>
        <h2>Separate acquisition promise from reorder timing</h2>
        <p>Build a brewing-guide form and a discount form, then confirm each subscriber receives the promised content. Test the built-in capture options in both candidates before retaining a separate popup subscription. If Privy captures while Drip sends, prove the exact source and preference transfer route, including retries. Do not infer that integration behavior from a generic connector badge.</p>
        <h2>Audit a sale-week edit with another operator</h2>
        <p>Ask a colleague to change only the large-bag delay and exclude a clearance collection. Have them explain which contacts already waiting will be affected, then inspect those contacts. Keep a before-and-after rule record and restore the original policy. Record time, errors and outside assistance; do not label either interface easier before this exercise.</p>
        <h2>Compare economics without invented recovery results</h2>
        <p>Use the same eligible subscriber count, planned sends and support expectations when requesting quotes. Count the effort to update product mappings and review exclusions. In a hypothetical workload, two rule changes at two hours each plus three hours of monthly review equal seven hours. At an assumed $50 per hour that is $350 of internal effort, before software or message charges; it is not evidence of recovered revenue.</p>
        <h2>Choose around maintainable policy</h2>
        <p>Choose the candidate that expresses the tested reorder rules with reliable data and an owner who can explain changes. Keep both only if capture contributes a demonstrated benefit that outweighs connector work. Order count and signup rate are useful business measurements, but neither creates a universal migration threshold. Preserve the old journey definition until queued messages and purchase cancellation have been reconciled.</p>
        <h2>An additional email candidate</h2>
        <p>Sequenzy may be worth evaluating if neither implementation fits the team. Use the same gift, subscription and repeat-order fixtures; require evidence for event availability and cancellation behavior. Do not assume guided playbooks remove the need to maintain catalog rules or guarantee better retention.</p>
        <h2>Product references and limits</h2>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy product scope</a> · <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/integrations">Privy connectors</a> · <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/drip">Drip Shopify listing</a>. Product documentation establishes advertised scope, not your account configuration, comparative performance or a guaranteed implementation. Privy also offers expert assistance; confirm the actual work included rather than assuming managed execution.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-drip">Omnisend vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-privy">Omnisend vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/food-and-beverage">Email apps for food &amp; beverage</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Drip FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
